# Absurdly Accurate Clock — Consumer Edition Requirements

## Purpose

AAC-CE is the consumer/appliance-oriented derivative of the original
Absurdly Accurate Clock.

It inherits the proven AAC GPS/PPS timing architecture, but is intended
to behave like an exceptionally good normal household clock rather than
a laboratory/development instrument.

The original AAC remains the reference/development platform.

---

## 1. Timing Architecture

The existing AAC timing model remains fundamental:

GPS UTC label + GPS PPS
        ↓
qualification / association
        ↓
authoritative UTC state
        ↓
consumer time policy
        ↓
presentation
        ↓
display backend

The CE display must never perform independent timekeeping.

Canonical internal time remains UTC.

GPS parsing, PPS processing, UTC/PPS association, qualification and
reacquisition logic should be inherited from the existing AAC unless
measured evidence requires a change.

---

## 2. MCU / Build Target

AAC-CE targets Raspberry Pi Pico 2 / RP2350 only. RP2350 is the selected
hardware for Dad's clock and the sole current PlatformIO target. Do not add
RP2040 compatibility layers, alternate toolchains, or build targets.

Platform-specific display transport and hardware support should remain outside
the clock timing core.

---

## 3. Time Quality States

CE should expose at least three meaningful time states:

### UNSYNCED

No authoritative UTC/PPS relationship has been established since boot.

The clock must not present an arbitrary or stale time as valid.

### LOCKED

UTC is currently qualified against GPS/PPS.

### HOLDOVER

HOLDOVER begins only after the clock was fully LOCKED and qualified GPS/PPS
timing is lost. It is not available before the first successful qualification.

During HOLDOVER, the clock continues advancing its local time estimate using
the RP2350 crystal-derived timebase. The duration is measured from the loss of
qualified timing and represents one continuous loss interval. Brief GPS data,
a satellite-count improvement, an isolated PPS edge, or partial qualification
does not clear the condition or restart its timer. Only successful completion
of the established GPS/PPS qualification rules returns the clock to LOCKED and
clears holdover state and duration. This applies both before and after expiry.

The qualified HOLDOVER window lasts 24 continuous hours. If normal
requalification has not completed by then, enter HOLDOVER_EXPIRED. Continue
displaying and counting the best available time estimate; do not blank the
clock or stop its timebase. From expiry onward, display that estimate as
explicitly unqualified/best-effort. A reset or power interruption does not
preserve holdover without additional hardware. Never represent estimated local
time as GPS/PPS-verified.

HOLDOVER_EXPIRED remains active until full GPS/PPS requalification succeeds.
Partial observations never reset the 24-hour interval. Successful
requalification returns to LOCKED, clears the expired/holdover condition, and
restores the live health presentation.

This state machine and 24-hour transition are requirements, not implemented
behavior. The inherited AAC timing engine invalidates UTC on reference loss and
does not extrapolate local time. Accuracy during future holdover depends on the
Pico 2 oscillator and operating conditions; no guaranteed accuracy may be
claimed without measurement.

