## 6. Production Personalization and Development Builds

Production personalization direction:

- timezone is a build-time choice, not a persistent user setting. The physical
  clock face is always 12-hour, with no leading zero and `12` at midnight and
  noon.

The development profile supports deterministic simulated GPS/PPS for bench
development without attached GPS hardware. Simulated input must use the same
parsers, reception association, qualification, and authoritative UTC state as
real GPS/PPS. Do not mix development behavior into production by default.

Stage 2A implements two compile-time PlatformIO profiles: `pico2` for
production and `pico2-dev` for development. They use the same Pico 2 / RP2350
target, firmware architecture, dependencies, and display abstraction. The
profile selector is independent of compile-time display selection. Both
standard profiles default to STDOUT; optional physical drivers are selected
explicitly. `include/ce_config.hpp`
centralizes separate typed production/development profile settings and
presentation defaults. Current configured behavior remains UTC and 24-hour
time; birthday/holiday data and brightness/current limits are unset pending
user-provided dates and hardware measurements.

Production `pico2` selects only real UART0/PPS input; the simulator implementation
and synthetic time settings are excluded from that profile. Development
`pico2-dev` selects simulated input only and does not initialize GPS UART or
register the physical PPS interrupt. It reports `GPS_SOURCE=SIMULATED`, while
production reports `GPS_SOURCE=REAL`; source identity remains distinct from
timebase qualification state.

The development simulator starts after display initialization. Its first
nominal PPS occurs one second later and carries the configured seed label
2026-01-01 00:00:00 UTC. PPS repeats every 1,000,000 µs. The RMC begins 200 ms
after its associated pulse and is delivered at the 9600-baud serial byte
cadence, followed by valid GGA satellite data. These deterministic timestamps
use the monotonic timer, not host wall time. Events more than 20 ms late are
dropped and invalidate the current association rather than being replayed with
fabricated past timestamps. The first seed label does not imply valid time:
normal cadence, consecutive-label, and following-pulse qualification must still
establish lock.

### Development USB monitor and console

Implemented in Stage 2C.1 for `pico2-dev` only. The development firmware boots
in Monitor Mode and streams asynchronous diagnostics. Press Enter to enter
Console Mode and receive the `aac>` prompt. Console input is echoed and accepts
backspace/delete, CR, LF, and CRLF. The available commands are:

- `help` — list commands and syntax.
- `status` — inspect source, qualification flags, valid authoritative UTC,
  simulated receiver label, PPS/RMC details, satellite status, and dropped
  diagnostic/response byte counts.
- `time YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS` — restart the simulated receiver at the supplied
  UTC value. Valid years are 2000–2099, matching the existing RMC parser.
- `reset` — restart at the configured default seed, currently
  2026-01-01 00:00:00 UTC.
- `exit` — return to Monitor Mode.

`time` and `reset` clear the existing Timebase instance, parsers, pending
sentences, PPS sequence, and poll timestamp tracking. They restart synthetic
input and require normal RMC/PPS qualification before authoritative UTC is
valid again. They do not directly set authoritative time or qualification
flags.

While in Console Mode, diagnostic events continue to be generated but
unsolicited output is suppressed. Diagnostic history is bounded to 1024 bytes;
command responses have a separate bounded 512-byte queue and priority. Queued
diagnostics are discarded on mode transitions, so exiting does not dump old
records. A slow or disconnected USB terminal cannot block clock servicing.
The development console implementation is excluded from production `pico2`,
which retains asynchronous diagnostics only. Stage 2C.1 host tests and physical
Pico 2 console verification are complete. Stage 2C.2 physical fault-injection
verification remains pending.

### Development GPS/PPS fault injection

Implemented in Stage 2C.2 for `pico2-dev` only. The console accepts:

- `fault status`
- `fault gps off|on`
- `fault pps off|on`
- `fault rmc bad-checksum|early|late|normal`
- `fault clear`

GPS-message output, delivered PPS output, and RMC mode are independent receiver
controls. Defaults are GPS ON, PPS ON, and RMC NORMAL. Settings persist until
changed or the development firmware restarts. `fault status` reports these
receiver controls; the separate `status` command continues to report the actual
qualified timebase state. Invalid fault commands report usage and do not alter
the controls.

GPS OFF suppresses synthetic RMC and GGA bytes while the receiver's internal
one-second UTC schedule and enabled PPS output continue. GPS ON resumes with
the current receiver label; the timebase must recover through its normal
freshness and qualification rules. PPS OFF suppresses delivered edges but
leaves GPS sentences and receiver UTC scheduling active. PPS ON emits only
future scheduled edges, with no catch-up pulses; ordinary cadence and UTC
qualification must reacquire. `fault clear` restores the three receiver
defaults without resetting or qualifying the timebase. Existing `time` and
`reset` receiver reseeding preserves the selected fault controls and clears
pending synthetic events.

BAD-CHECKSUM changes the generated RMC checksum and sends the record through the
normal UART-byte and NMEA-parser path; GGA remains available. EARLY schedules
the RMC start at 5 ms after PPS (first byte event at 6,041 µs after PPS), before
the 20 ms association minimum. LATE schedules the RMC start at 880 ms; the CR
completion event occurs at 916,458 µs after PPS, beyond the 900 ms limit but
before the next pulse. GGA is omitted in LATE mode so 9,600-baud sentences do
not overlap the next one-second cycle. The simulator retains its 20 ms event
servicing-lateness safeguard. These are deterministic simulated faults, not
physical receiver or GPS-accuracy measurements. Physical Pico 2 verification
of the Stage 2C.2 console and fault behavior remains pending.

Acquisition/recovery demonstrations and calendar/theme test scenarios remain
future work.

Authoritative UTC must never be restored from persistent storage and falsely
treated as GPS-qualified after a cold boot.

