## 9. Display Architecture

Presentation should consume a coherent snapshot of clock state.

Conceptually:

ClockCore
    ↓
ConsumerPolicy
    ↓
PresentationState
    ↓
DisplayBackend
    ↓
PhysicalDisplay

Display code must not:

- qualify GPS
- associate UTC with PPS
- advance authoritative UTC
- decide whether the clock is locked
- implement independent holdover timekeeping

Drivers are selected and composed at compile time; no runtime plugin system is
required. With no explicit selection, STDOUT is selected automatically.
Selecting one or more physical drivers does not silently add STDOUT; select
`AAC_DISPLAY_STDOUT` explicitly to combine it with physical drivers. All
selected drivers consume the same coherent presentation snapshot. The generic
framework performs static composition only; it contains no device protocol,
rendering, or hardware ownership. Each driver owns its formatting, buffers,
cadence, transport, initialization, and bounded nonblocking service. A driver
must not access or alter authoritative time. The WS2812 driver provides raw
pixel access and logical 32x8 mapping for the observed vertical serpentine
column topology. The four-corner, perimeter, and row/column geometry proofs are
physically verified. A temporary 5x7 numeric font and presentation-state
`HH:MM` renderer are implemented; live clock-mode behavior is physically
verified by the user. MAX7219 and TFT drivers are not implemented.

The optional PD-2200 driver retains its verified Noritake-compatible command
behavior. For the verified PD-2200, command `0x0E` clears displayed characters
without resetting the cursor/write position; `0x0C` homes it. This behavior
must not be assumed for other Noritake-capable displays. The presentation
adapter, PD-2200 command driver, and UART1/RS-232 transport are distinct layers.

The framework supports multiple selected drivers sharing one presentation
snapshot. Different technologies and refresh rates can be composed; each
driver must keep service bounded so one output does not hold up timing or other
drivers. Current implemented drivers are STDOUT, optional PD-2200 VFD, and
optional WS2812. The WS2812 8x32 Panel remains selected consumer hardware; its
final logical clock renderer depends on physical verification of corrected
corner rendering.

### Future multi-display composition

AAC-CE must accommodate future configurations with multiple heterogeneous
display backends operating simultaneously. Different mechanisms may present
different parts of the same clock presentation: for example, a robotic arm for
hours, an LCD for minutes, an LED matrix for seconds, a VFD for date or other
information, and independent status indicators for clock quality. These are
possible configurations, not hardware selected for Dad's build.

The authoritative GPS/PPS timing engine remains independent of every display.
Multiple backends may consume the same coherent, read-only presentation state,
with responsibilities assigned by each mechanism's capabilities and needs.
Backends may have different update frequencies, refresh policies, response
times, and hardware capabilities. Slow or electromechanical mechanisms must
not block authoritative timekeeping or unrelated display operations; future
backends may operate asynchronously where needed. The shared presentation
model must not require every backend to support a framebuffer, pixels, RGB,
characters, or instantaneous updates. Physical behavior and transport remain
backend responsibilities, and display composition must never create another
authoritative source of time.

Compile-time composition and shared snapshot delivery are implemented. Rich
responsibility routing and asynchronous scheduling are not; each selected
driver currently receives the full snapshot and services once per application
pass. Dad's selected display remains one WS2812 8x32 Panel.

Display failure must not corrupt clock state.

